# Drafted by Gemini (R20), edited. R20: the crime scene in the post office, the master stroke, and the envelopes.

~ false_geneva
quest roving_rookery crime_scene
Narrator: The post office lamp stands cold and unlit above a scatter of fallen letters. Behind the counter lies what remains of Theodora Mayville, while little Geneva clings to the doorjamb.
geneva_fairchild [afraid]: Please, you have to look at poor Miss Theodora. I tried to go near the counter, truly I did, but my legs won't carry me another step.
* Tell us everything you saw, Geneva. -> geneva_story
* Step behind the counter and examine the body. -> body_look
* Study Geneva closely. -> accuse
* Back away from the counter. -> stroke

~ geneva_story
geneva_fairchild [sad]: I came in at daybreak to sort the morning sacks and saw her lying there. I didn't touch a single letter, I swear it, I only ran for you.
Narrator: Her tale comes out smooth as a river pebble, though the dusty floorboards where she claims she stood bear not a single footprint.
-> false_geneva

~ body_look
Narrator: A woman lies crumpled behind the sorting table, yet her shape is all wrong, folded flat like a heavy winter coat dropped from its peg.
* [Medicine DC 15] Check the remains for wounds, bone, and blood. -> body_skin | body_missed
* [Investigation DC 15] Search the floorboards for tracks, feathers, or spilled ink. -> body_skin | body_missed
* Step back from the counter. -> false_geneva

~ body_skin
set rk_skin_known
Narrator: There is no bone here, nor flesh, nor a drop of blood. Someone has laid out the postmaster's empty, eyeless skin like a cast-off garment.
geneva_fairchild [afraid]: I can't bear to look at her, I really can't. I'll just wait right here by the latch until you're done.
-> false_geneva

~ body_missed
Narrator: It is a ruin of a woman, cold and dreadful in the gloom, and your eyes can make no further sense of it.
-> false_geneva

~ accuse
Narrator: You turn from the counter and fix your gaze on the small clerk by the door.
geneva_fairchild [afraid]: Why are you staring at me like that? I'm the one who fetched you, aren't I?
-> stroke

~ stroke
cutscene rk_master_stroke
geneva_fairchild [angry]: Murderers! Help, they've slain poor Theodora right before my eyes! Oh, look at your faces, my sweet gulls, walked right into the coop!
cutscene rk_master_stroke_2
Narrator: She folds inward with a dry rush of grey feathers, and a second empty skin settles softly onto the floorboards where the girl just stood.
cutscene rk_master_stroke_3
Narrator: Beyond the north window, the great dovecote rises on scaly pigeon legs, spreads broad wings of thatch, and lurches into the dark above the roofs.
cutscene end
set golub_exposed
set hysteria_began = now
set rk_posse_out
quest roving_rookery posse
Narrator: From the Founder's Round comes the roar of a dozen angry voices, followed by the heavy, oncoming stamp of boots down the lane.
move wickermoor_village:from_post_office

~ skin_theodora
Narrator: Theodora Mayville's hollow skin lies slack as a shed coat on the boards, its eye sockets empty, reeking of old straw and wet feathers.
* [Medicine DC 15] Examine the skin for wounds, bones, and how it was shed. -> skin_known | skin_unknown
* Leave the skin where it lies. -> END
~ skin_known
set rk_skin_known
Narrator: The hide was worn like a coat for years and slipped off whole at the seams, scored inside by something with hooked claws.
-> END
~ skin_unknown
Narrator: It is a body and yet no body at all, and you cannot fathom how such a thing could be.
-> END

~ skin_geneva
Narrator: A second skin lies crumpled by the threshold, small and fine-boned, its lilac eyes gone. Real Geneva Fairchild could not have been past twenty.
-> END

~ letters
Narrator: A tray of crisp blank envelopes sits beside an open pot of black ink, while a grey pigeon on the window rail tilts its head at you.
* Address an envelope to Theodora Mayville. -> letters_take
* Leave the envelopes alone. -> END
~ letters_take
give rk_letter_theodora
set rk_letter_ready
Narrator: You write the postmaster's name in a neat, steady hand and press the wax seal; any post bird that takes it will fly straight to its mistress.
-> END
